INT. UNIVERSITY BOATHOUSE - ERG ROOM - 5 A.M.

A long low room of rowing machines in rows. Windows fogged. A whiteboard with names and splits. The air smells of rubber and sweat.

Two rowers sit at adjacent machines, cooling down. COACH NADEZHDA PROKOFIEVA (50s, tracksuit, thermos, calm as a stopwatch) stands at the whiteboard capping a marker.

LEOPOLD "LEO" ARMSTRONG-DIOP (20s, stroke seat, shoulders like a door) wipes his face with a towel and doesn't speak.

The other rower, a stranger to the room, rests his hands on his knees. A STRANGER IN A SCARLET UNIFORM (20s, rival college crest, lean and quiet).

NADEZHDA
Leo. Splits.

LEO
Two-oh-one.

NADEZHDA
Pace?

LEO
Thirty-two.

NADEZHDA
Good.

She writes it. Leo glances at the Stranger, who has not looked up since he sat down.

LEO
Coach, who's this?

NADEZHDA
A guest.

LEO
Guest of who?

NADEZHDA
Of me.

Leo's eyes narrow. The Stranger lifts his head, finally. He has an easy, friendly face.

STRANGER
Morning.

LEO
You're from Harlow.

STRANGER
I'm from a lot of places.

LEO
Your kit says Harlow.

STRANGER
It's a good kit.

Nadezhda caps the marker, sets it in the tray, and takes a sip from her thermos. She has the air of a woman watching two dogs decide whether to be friends.

NADEZHDA
Leo, this is Kofi. He rows for Harlow. He's transferring.

LEO
Transferring where?

NADEZHDA
Here.

A silence. Leo lowers the towel slowly. He looks at the whiteboard, at the splits. His name is at the top. Under it is another name, written in a different hand and wiped out.

KOFI
Don't worry. I'm not here for your seat.

LEO
Then what are you here for?

KOFI
Seven seat. I row in the middle.

LEO
We have a seven seat.

KOFI
You have a seven seat that can't hold a pace.

LEO
That's Marcus. He's my roommate.

KOFI
I know. I'm sorry.

Leo gets up. The erg handle slides back with a clack. He stands in front of the Stranger, jaw working. Kofi stays seated, hands still.

NADEZHDA
Sit down, Leo.

LEO
I'm fine.

NADEZHDA
Sit down.

He sits. The machine sighs. Outside, geese take off from the river in a bark of wings.

LEO
(low)
Does Marcus know?

NADEZHDA
Marcus knows.

LEO
When did he find out?

NADEZHDA
Yesterday.

LEO
He didn't say.

NADEZHDA
He asked me not to let you hear it from him.

Leo shuts his eyes. Draws a long breath through his nose. Wipes his face with the towel, then wipes it again, though there's nothing left to wipe.

Kofi leans forward.

KOFI
I'd rather have rowed beside him, honestly.

LEO
(without opening his eyes)
Don't.

KOFI
I mean it.

LEO
I said don't.

Nadezhda crosses to the nearest erg and takes a seat. She straps in her feet. She looks like she means to row.

NADEZHDA
Both of you. Twenty minutes. Steady state. Twenty-two.

LEO
Coach...

NADEZHDA
That's not a request.

Leo opens his eyes. Looks at Kofi. Kofi holds the look, and then, slowly, offers a hand. Leo looks at the hand. Takes it. They shake once, stiff, like men sealing a bad bargain.

KOFI
Twenty-two?

LEO
Twenty-two.

They turn to their machines and begin to row. The chains whir. The fly wheels whisper. Nadezhda pulls her first stroke, then settles into the rhythm.

NADEZHDA
(softly, as if to herself)
Better.

FADE OUT.
